<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diego Silang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1730">1730</a>–<a href="/years/1763">1763</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Diego Silang was the leader of a revolt in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilocos">Ilocos</a> region of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a> against Spanish rule during the British occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a>. Declaring himself a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the British, he took Vigan in 1762, but he was assassinated in May 1763 by men in Spanish pay. His wife, Gabriela Silang, continued the revolt until she was captured and executed.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Diego Silang y Andaya was born on 16 December 1730 in Aringay, then part of Pangasinan and now in La Union. His father was Pangasinense and his mother Ilocano. He was baptised on 7 January 1731 in Vigan, in the then undivided province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilocos">Ilocos</a>, where as a boy he worked as a messenger for a local Castilian priest. Fluent in Spanish, he carried correspondence from the Ilocos to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a>, journeys on which he first saw the injustices of colonial rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> was allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a>, and the British seizure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> in October 1762 inspired risings in the far north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a>. Silang at first wanted to replace Spanish functionaries in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilocos">Ilocos</a> with native-born officials and offered to lead Ilocano forces on the Spanish side. The Spanish administrators instead transferred their powers to the Catholic Bishop of Nueva Segovia, who rejected the offer. The revolt drew on grievances over Spanish taxation and abuses and on Silang's belief that church and government in the Ilocos should be led by trained Ilocano officials.</p><p>Joaquín Martínez de Zúñiga, writing in 1805, gave a hostile account. He called Silang very quick and artful and said he persuaded the chiefs and many commoners, especially those of Vigan, that to keep the Catholic faith they must arm against the Spaniards and deliver them to the English. Armed men demanded that the alcalde give up his staff of government to the vicar-general; he did so and escaped from the province. The rebels then demanded of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>-elect, a Dominican named Ustariz, an exemption from the tribute, while Silang gathered troops for wider operations.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Silang was killed on 28 May 1763, aged 32, by a friend, a Spanish-Ilocano mestizo named Miguel Vicos, whom church authorities had paid and who was helped by Pedro Becbec. Gabriela Silang was forced to retreat to what is now Abra, led her troops towards Vigan and was driven back; she and her men were hanged on 20 September 1763, she last of all. The Philippine Navy has named two frigates after Diego Silang, one in service from 1976 to 1990 and one that entered service in 2025.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Diego Silang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The occupation encouraged uprisings, notably that of <b>Diego Silang</b> in Ilocos, who sought British support and was assassinated in 1763.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-occupation-of-manila">British occupation of Manila</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Aringay, in what is now <a href="/countries/philippines">the Philippines</a>. Died in Bantay, in what is now <a href="/countries/philippines">the Philippines</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=16.4,120.36&amp;year=1751" aria-label="Aringay on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7828 1554 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Diego Silang"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8278 1824h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8278 1824h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=16.4,120.36&amp;year=1751">What was here in 1751? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
